With increased access to biomedical and electronic health records data and the
complexity of research questions, individuals in residency programmes who aim to
conduct research require specialized educational programmes and biostatistics
support. Biostatistics collaboration units in academic health centres often work with
residents to conduct data-intensive research. These units face numerous challenges
related to providing training in statistical literacy and collaborating on resident-led
research within very restricted timelines. Since 2019, the Duke Biostatistics,
Epidemiology, and Research Design (BERD) Methods Core has supported over
247 resident-led projects by developing tools and resources to address these
challenges. This manuscript presents novel processes and training materials that
other institutions can use to help biostatistics collaboration units effectively support
resident training programmes. We provide a framework to support the development
of collaborative teams, along with specialized training materials for residents who
collaborate with these teams.
KEYWORDS
biostatistics collaboration, collaboration resources, collaborative process, resident training, resident-led research, team science
1 | INTRODUCTION
Building collaborative research teams and incorporating research training into residency programmes within academic health centres are essential
for enhancing career opportunities and fostering innovation within evolving healthcare landscapes (Anderson, 2006; Hurst et al., 2019; Institute
of Medicine, 2004). Engaging in research improves satisfaction with the residency experience and aids in developing future leaders in academic
medicine (Takahashi et al., 2009). Individuals in residency programmes engaged in research as part of their training can benefit from collaborative
research experiences and tailored learning to advance their career goals (Khalil et al., 2022; Permar et al., 2020). Academic health centres must
provide the necessary tools and support to facilitate this (Blanchard et al., 2018; Price Rapoza et al., 2022; Welty et al., 2013).
