Medical professionals need a fundamental understanding of research design and analytics, which is crucial for evidence-based medicine
(Arias et al., 2017; Oster & Enders, 2018; Sackett et al., 1996). Key competencies include identifying the strengths and limitations of study
designs, recognizing when to involve a quantitative expert, and appreciating reproducible research (Enders, 2011; Enders et al., 2017; Oster
et al., 2015; Oster & Enders, 2018). However, many residents lack the statistical knowledge and confidence to interpret study results
(Araoye et al., 2020; Best & Laskin, 2013; Bookstaver et al., 2012; Couture et al., 2020; Susarla & Redett, 2014; Windish et al., 2007). Biostatistics
collaboration units that typically partner biostatisticians with investigators conducting research must account for these gaps in knowledge when
developing processes to effectively collaborate with residents.
With increased access to biomedical and electronic health records data and the complexity of research questions, residents must have access
to biostatistics collaboration and statistical literacy training. The biostatistician in the collaboration provides expertise in choosing the study
design, sample size, and endpoints that can answer these complex research questions, and this guidance provides training for the rest of the team
through example. Without biostatistics collaboration and statistical literacy training, projects may lack sufficiently developed research questions,
rigour, reproducibility and statistical analyses (Bland, 2004; Brown et al., 2018; Gunel Karadeniz et al., 2019). Despite several proposed curricula
for statistical education (Arias et al., 2017; McLaughlin & Kang, 2017; Windish, 2011), a gap persists in covering fundamental competencies,
particularly statistical collaboration and reproducible research (Oster et al., 2020). In addition, collaboration units must work under the time
constraints of resident trainees, who often need to assimilate their learning to develop data-intensive research questions rapidly.
This manuscript provides the foundational framework to address the problem of developing biostatistics collaboration teams dedicated to
supporting resident training programmes. This work aims to support biostatistics collaboration units in overcoming two main challenges with
resident-led research: (1) building a biostatistics team to mentor, train, and collaborate with residents on projects under time constraints and
(2) training residents in clinical research to facilitate collaboration with the biostatistics team. We do this by reviewing the processes and
training material developed by the Duke Biostatistics, Epidemiology, and Research Design (BERD) Methods Core, which has supported over
247 resident-led research projects. First, we describe the collaborative team model the BERD Core uses to support resident-led research. Next,
we provide a roadmap for the collaboration process and template documents that support collaboration between residents and biostatisticians.
Finally, we present flexible training modules that can be used to help residents better engage with biostatisticians on data-intensive research
projects. We aim to provide biostatistics collaboration units with tools and training materials to facilitate engagement with resident-led research
and to train residents in clinical research and collaboration.
2 | BIOSTATISTICS COLLABORATION TEAM TO SUPPORT FOR RESIDENTS
The BERD Core comprises over 40 staff and faculty biostatisticians and data scientists who partner with more than 20 research groups in the
Duke School of Medicine and Duke University Health System (Desai et al., 2022). These partnerships are with Duke clinical research units such as
the Global Health Institute, the Center for AIDS Research and the Department of Pharmacy, Neurosurgery, Pediatrics, among others. Biostatisti
cians in the BERD Core have partnered with residents across the School of Medicine on more than 247 data-driven research projects, resulting in
over 77 publications since 2019. Funding for a biostatistician's effort may come from external grants, but often for resident-led research, biosta
tistics effort is supported by internal departmental funding.
We use the BERD Core's longest standing collaboration dedicated to supporting resident-led research as a case illustration of a successful
biostatistics collaboration team. The collaboration with Duke University Hospital's Department of Pharmacy began in 2012 and has supported
more than 100 resident-led projects since 2017. The Department of Pharmacy's residency programme consists of a generalized first-year
programme, followed by an optional second year programme focusing on a pharmacy-specific specialty practice area. Residents in both years are
required to complete a research project starting in July with the goal of dissemination by the following June. Consequently, these projects often
need to occur simultaneously, posing a significant challenge for partnering biostatisticians. We have found that managing this workload is most
effective when distributed across multiple biostatisticians.
In 2022, the Department of Pharmacy requested collaboration for 18 residents. The BERD Core found it optimal to divide the work among
five staff biostatisticians, each contributing 15%–25% effort with varying levels of expertise and experience, one faculty member at 30%–40%
effort, as well as two student interns who worked flexibly up to 20 h a week based on project needs. This allocation was determined by annual
assessment of the residency programme's requirements and iteratively optimizing effort allocations to align with the group's needs. Moreover, at
the start of the residency year, the residency programme leaders coordinate with the biostatistics team to assess the timelines and complexity of
all projects so that projects can be effectively assigned to biostatisticians. The goal is to allocate effort to ensure that each biostatistician has a
diverse range of projects with varying complexity and deadlines. It also enhances efficiency to assign projects with similar methods or clinical
domains to the same biostatistician.
