3 | A ROADMAP FOR THE COLLABORATION PROCESS
Once appropriate biostatistics effort is in place, it is important to establish a clear and transparent timeline for residents and biostatisticians to fol
low. Appendix S1 presents the ‘roadmap’ that was developed to support a 12-month residency programme. This roadmap includes 11 steps that
residents and biostatisticians take in order to reach the project goals within the established timeframe. The first step is for residents to meet with
the biostatisticians on the team to discuss the study design for protocol development. In Step 2, biostatisticians provide documentation and a
workshop on data collection using reproducible databases (e.g., Harris et al., 2009, 2019), which facilitates the third step, created to ensure resi
dents know how to structure data collection in order to optimize collaboration with a biostatistician. The roadmap then specifies when data col
lection should begin and end, provides guidance for developing a statistical analysis plan and ends with a reminder to include biostatisticians as
co-authors. Monthly meetings between the biostatistics team and residency programme leadership helps to ensure residents adhere to project
timelines and resolve any issues promptly.
To help residents navigate the roadmap presented in Appendix S1, four templates were created for protocol development, data collection,
statistical analysis plans and statistical reports. Appendix S2 provides a template that guides residents through submitting a protocol to the Duke
Institutional Review Board. The study protocol includes data dictionaries which outline how measures will be collected and from which data
sources. Appendix S3 provides a template data dictionary that is given to the residents who then use it to develop their database in Step 3 of the
roadmap. Biostatisticians provide hands on support to residents as they complete these templates.
Once the residents have identified and described the data they intend to collect, finalized their protocols, and built their databases, biostatisti
cians will develop an agreed-upon statistical analysis plan (SAP) aligned with the research project's goals. Since biostatisticians are typically the pri
mary author of SAPs, a template for this document was initially developed to train junior biostatisticians (Pomann et al., 2020). Appendix S4
presents a revised template designed specifically to help pharmacy residents better understand the SAPs that biostatisticians develop. In addition
to a SAP template, Appendix S5 provides an analytic report template that is shared with residents to provide them with a better understanding of
how the results will be presented when the biostatistician completes the analyses.
4 | JUST IN TIME TRAINING MATERIALS FOR RESIDENTS
In addition to the roadmap and templates presented, the BERD Core provides residents with optional just-in-time training materials that are pub
licly available (https://bit.ly/introtores). The BERD Core designed these training materials to fill curricular gaps in residency education. Notably,
the educational content emphasizes the value of reproducible research and biostatistics collaboration (Oster et al., 2020). The content also
encourages engagement by being problem-based, relevant, and applicable to clinical decision-making.
The BERD Core collaborated with instructional designers to align the design of the materials with best practices for digital learning today.
The online modules are intentionally brief to minimize interference with residents' demanding clinical training schedules. Also, the material allows
for easy pairing of online content with customizable in-person workshops tailored specifically to the clinical discipline of the audience. Lastly, the
materials are well-organized, succinct, readily available, and can be accessed in a just-in-time manner as needed. The following subsections
describe the content and results from a study to evaluate the materials.
4.1 | Training curriculum content and format
The four publicly available online modules introduce fundamental concepts of clinical research. Table 1 provides detailed information about what
each module covers on four topics: (1) Introduction to Research and Design, (2) Formulating the Research Question, (3) Null and Alternative
Hypotheses and (4) Study Design and Data Collection. Each module ranges from 10 to 20 min in length, with the total time commitment for all
four being approximately 1 h. This online content was designed to teach fundamental competencies in an asynchronous, standalone format,
enabling learners to access it in a self-directed manner as needed. Residents can watch these modules at any time before, during or after their
research projects begin. The content can also be easily paired with in-person workshops that go into more depth about specific case studies that
residents are currently working on or are interested in learning about.
