Participants were asked about the number of modules watched, and if all four were not viewed, the reason for not doing so. Participants were
also asked to rate the modules' length, the information's relevancy and satisfaction with the content.
Twenty participants responded to the survey. Of these, 14 (70%) were residents, and six (30%) were non-residents. The response rate among
residents was 52% (14 of 27); however, we could not assess the overall survey response rate because the clinical leaders shared the curriculum
and survey with select medical students and this activity was not tracked. Sixteen respondents (80%) reported having more than a little amount of
prior experience conducting clinical research. Eighteen participants (90%) watched all four modules, while one (5%) watched three modules, and
one (5%) watched no modules. The only reported reason for not watching all four modules was lack of time. Among the 18 participants who pro
vided feedback, most (89%) said the modules were just the right length, all 18 (100%) found the content somewhat or very relevant and 14 (78%)
were somewhat or extremely satisfied with the content (Table 2).
5 | DISCUSSION
Biostatistics collaboration units in academic healthcare settings need to support research training when partnering with residents. Residents can
influence scientific progress and healthcare improvement by conducting data-intensive research in partnership with biostatisticians. Cultivating
critical evaluation skills for published studies and establishing effective collaborations with quantitative experts is crucial for advancing evidence
based medicine and public health. This manuscript presents resources and training materials that collaboration units can use to help residents gain
these skills and conduct meaningful research.
A primary challenge in supporting resident-led research within a biostatistics collaboration unit is establishing the right team with sufficient
funding and flexible effort allocations to meet the requirements of the residency programme. While it is ideal for residents to be partnered with
clinical or scientific mentors possessing research funding to address the quantitative collaboration needs, the number of residents aiming to do
research will undoubtedly outnumber available funded projects for them to lead. Even if funding is in place, it can be challenging for biostatisti
cians to allocate effort effectively. This challenge is mitigated by having multiple biostatisticians who can work together to efficiently split the
effort.
Another major challenge is the limited amount of time residents have for research activities. The BERD Core has addressed this challenge by
developing a clear roadmap of the collaborative process with specific timelines for project milestones and regular check-ins with leadership to
ensure that projects remain on target. In addition, we provide the residents with just-in-time online training modules that facilitate the collabora
tive process by introducing them to fundamental principles and limitations in clinical research and statistical competencies at a high level, rather
