AUTHOR BIOGRAPHIES
Clemontina A. Davenport is a Senior Biostatistician in Biostatistics, Epidemiology and Research Design (BERD) Methods Core and Course
Director in the Department of Biostatistics and Bioinformatics at Duke University, Durham, NC. In these roles, she has provided statistical
leadership in clinical research studies and taught, trained and mentored over 250 biostatistics graduate students and staff.
Hui-Jie Lee is a Senior Biostatistician of the Biostatistics, Epidemiology, and Research Design (BERD) Methods Core at Duke University,
Durham, NC. She leads the statistical collaborations with the Department of Neurosurgery, Department of Pharmacy, and Marcus Center for
Cellular Cures.
Quentin Ruiz-Esparza is the Director of Digital Product Strategy and Design for Learning Innovation and Lifetime Education at Duke Univer
sity (https://learninginnovation.duke.edu/). Quentin works with Duke's schools and academic units in developing strategy for digital learning
in professional and lifetime education as well as oversees a team of learning experience (LX) designers and producers who partner with Duke
faculty to design digital courses and programs.
Nicholas Janes is a Learning Media Designer with Learning Innovation and Lifetime Learning at Duke University, Durham, NC
(https://learninginnovation.duke.edu/). Nicholas works and collaborates with Duke's media production team to conceptualize and create visu
ally engaging course videos.
Megan L. Neely is a faculty member in the Biostatistics and Bioinformatics Department and in the Duke Clinical Research Institute at Duke
University Medical Center. Her research focuses on survival and longitudinal analysis with applications in pulmonary disease. She has taught
several courses in the Master of Biostatistics and Clinical Research Training Programs.
Lacey Rende is the Project Planner for the Biostatistics, Epidemiology and Research Design (BERD) Methods Core at Duke University, a data
science unit of over 50 student, staff and faculty biostatisticians and data scientists who collaborate on biomedical research projects with
clinical and translational researchers across the Duke University School of Medicine.
Gregory P. Samsa is a Professor of Biostatistics and Bioinformatics and co-Director of its Master of Biostatistics program. An experienced
collaborative biostatistician, his primary research interest is in statistical pedagogy.
